@article{ WOS:001851587400001,
Author = {Hitchcock, Kelly and Moss, Andrew and Yarnell, Richard W. and Counsell,
   Gregory and Williams, Leah J. and Hamill, Kat and Tollington, Simon},
Title = {Barriers and opportunities for further enhancing urban garden
   biodiversity: Insights from a behaviour change model for the United
   Kingdom},
Journal = {PEOPLE AND NATURE},
Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 AUG 18},
Abstract = {Urbanisation poses a major threat to biodiversity, yet residential
   gardens offer opportunities to provide key habitats for urban wildlife.
   Understanding both the perceived feasibility of implementing further
   improvements to gardens for biodiversity and the barriers that constrain
   engagement is essential for designing effective conservation
   interventions. This study uses a questionnaire survey of 231
   householders in Great Britain to assess the likelihood of adding 10
   wildlife-friendly garden features and to identify perceived barriers
   using a behaviour change framework which considers capability (C),
   opportunity (O) and motivation (M) as determinants of behaviour (B)
   (COM-B model) Within a sample of gardens, in which most already
   contained at least one wildlife-friendly feature, the overall odds of
   respondents implementing further improvements were low, indicating a
   37.5\% probability of engagement. Wildflower patches were the only
   feature rated as more likely than unlikely to be implemented, while
   compost heaps and ponds were the least likely. All COM-B barrier
   subgroups significantly reduced the likelihood of engagement, with
   physical opportunity constraints (67.9\%), particularly a lack of space,
   and psychological capability (20.5\%) being the most prevalent, followed
   by social opportunity (6.2\%) and physical capability (5.4\%). Younger
   respondents and those from more socio-economically deprived areas
   reported more barriers. Given that many surveyed gardens already
   contained wildlife-enhancing elements, limited space, the level of
   effort required, associated costs and existing knowledge were key
   obstacles to further improving gardens for biodiversity, even among a
   potentially conservation-aware group. Our findings indicate that the
   presence of existing conservation efforts does not necessarily predict
   householders' engagement with additional biodiversity interventions.
   Individuals report substantial barriers to engagement; therefore, we
   recommend that conservation non-governmental organisations (NGOs)
   coordinate efforts that target higher-level stakeholders, enabling
   resources to be reallocated towards addressing barriers through top-down
   policy change. Policy-led interventions have the potential to overcome
   barriers to individual householder engagement, thereby improving
   biodiversity outcomes at a broader spatial scale. Applying these
   insights in policy and practice could enhance urban gardens as key
   contributors to biodiversity conservation.Read the free for this article
   on the Journal blog.},
